D={}
def E(i,b='',v='',n=''): D[i]=dict(bloodSupply=b,venousDrainage=v,innervation=n)
CP="Autonomic only, through the superficial and deep cardiac plexuses. Sympathetic fibres (pre-ganglionic from spinal segments T1-T4/5, relayed in the cervical and upper thoracic sympathetic ganglia) raise heart rate and force of contraction. Parasympathetic fibres from the vagus nerve (X) slow the sinoatrial and atrioventricular nodes. Visceral afferents carry cardiac pain with the sympathetic nerves, which is why it is felt in the chest, neck and left arm."
E('heart',n=CP)
E('right-atrium',"Right coronary artery (atrial branches); the sinoatrial nodal artery arises from it in about 60% of people and from the left circumflex artery in the rest.","Anterior cardiac veins and the smallest (Thebesian) veins open directly into the atrium; the coronary sinus, which collects most cardiac veins, opens into it.","Cardiac plexus (sympathetic and vagal fibres); the sinoatrial node in its wall is the pacemaker, modulated by both. Visceral afferents travel with the sympathetic and vagal fibres.")
E('right-ventricle',"Right coronary artery (right marginal branch and the posterior interventricular branch in a right-dominant heart), plus small branches of the left anterior descending artery to the anterior wall next to the septum.","Anterior cardiac veins drain directly into the right atrium; the small cardiac vein runs to the coronary sinus.","Cardiac plexus: sympathetic fibres from T1-T4/5 and vagal parasympathetic fibres; the conduction fibres (right bundle branch, Purkinje network) are modulated by these nerves.")
E('left-atrium',"Left circumflex artery (atrial branches); the sinoatrial nodal artery arises from it in about 40% of people.","Small cardiac veins and the oblique vein of the left atrium (Marshall) drain to the coronary sinus. Blood does not drain through the four pulmonary veins; they bring oxygenated blood into the atrium.","Cardiac plexus (sympathetic and vagal fibres); volume (stretch) receptors in the atrial wall send afferents in the vagus nerve.")
E('left-ventricle',"Left anterior descending (anterior interventricular) artery and left circumflex artery (obtuse marginal branches) supply most of the wall; the posterior interventricular branch (from the right coronary artery in about 70-80% of hearts) supplies the inferior wall and posterior septum.","Great, middle and posterior cardiac veins drain to the coronary sinus; a small part drains through Thebesian veins directly into the cavity.","Cardiac plexus (sympathetic fibres from T1-T4/5, vagal parasympathetic fibres); mainly sympathetic control of contractility. Ischaemic pain is carried by sympathetic afferents.")
VALVE="Valve leaflets are thin and almost avascular: they are nourished by diffusion from the blood passing over them. Small vessels from the adjacent myocardium (coronary branches) reach only the base of the leaflets and the valve ring."
E('tricuspid-valve',VALVE+" The ring is supplied by the right coronary artery.","Any small vessels at the base of the leaflets drain to the anterior cardiac veins and the coronary sinus.","A sparse network of autonomic and sensory fibres from the cardiac plexus lies mostly at the base of the leaflets and the papillary muscles; the right ventricular conduction fibres run close by.")
E('pulmonary-valve',VALVE+" The ring and sinuses are supplied by branches of the right coronary artery (conus branch) and the left anterior descending artery.","Any small vessels at the base of the cusps drain to the anterior cardiac veins.","A sparse network of autonomic and sensory fibres from the cardiac plexus, mostly at the base of the cusps.")
E('mitral-valve',VALVE+" The ring is supplied by the left circumflex artery; the papillary muscles receive blood from the left anterior descending and the circumflex or right coronary arteries (the posteromedial one often from a single artery, so it is ischaemia-prone).","Any small vessels at the base of the leaflets drain to the great cardiac vein and the coronary sinus.","A sparse network of autonomic and sensory fibres from the cardiac plexus, mostly at the base of the leaflets and the papillary muscles.")
E('aortic-valve',VALVE+" The coronary arteries arise from the right and left aortic sinuses just above the valve.","Any small vessels at the base of the cusps drain to the great cardiac vein and the coronary sinus.","A sparse network of autonomic and sensory fibres from the cardiac plexus, mostly at the base of the cusps.")
E('interventricular-septum',"Anterior two-thirds: septal perforating branches of the left anterior descending artery. Posterior one-third: septal branches of the posterior interventricular artery. The atrioventricular nodal artery (usually from the right coronary artery) supplies the AV node at its base.","Great cardiac vein (anterior part) and middle cardiac vein (posterior part) to the coronary sinus.","Cardiac plexus (sympathetic and vagal fibres) modulates the bundle of His and its branches, which run in the septum.")
# respiratory
E('nasal-cavity',v="Sphenopalatine, anterior ethmoidal and facial veins; these drain to the pterygoid venous plexus, the facial vein and the superior ophthalmic vein (and so the cavernous sinus).")
E('pharynx',"Ascending pharyngeal artery (external carotid), ascending palatine and tonsillar branches of the facial artery, pharyngeal branches of the maxillary artery, and branches of the inferior thyroid artery.","Pharyngeal venous plexus on the outer surface of the pharynx, draining to the pterygoid plexus, the facial vein and the internal jugular vein.","Pharyngeal plexus (vagus nerve X with glossopharyngeal IX and sympathetic fibres from the superior cervical ganglion). Motor: vagus (cranial accessory fibres) to all pharyngeal muscles except stylopharyngeus (glossopharyngeal). Sensory: V2 for the nasopharynx, IX for the oropharynx, internal laryngeal nerve (X) for the laryngopharynx.")
E('larynx',"Superior laryngeal artery (from the superior thyroid) with the internal laryngeal nerve, and inferior laryngeal artery (from the inferior thyroid) with the recurrent laryngeal nerve.","Superior and inferior laryngeal veins to the superior thyroid vein (internal jugular) and the inferior thyroid vein (brachiocephalic vein).","Vagus nerve (X). Superior laryngeal nerve: internal branch is sensory above the vocal folds, external branch supplies cricothyroid. Recurrent laryngeal nerve: motor to all other intrinsic muscles and sensory below the vocal folds. Sympathetic fibres from the superior cervical ganglion.")
E('trachea',"Inferior thyroid arteries (tracheal branches) for the cervical part; bronchial arteries from the thoracic aorta for the lower part.","Inferior thyroid veins to the left brachiocephalic vein; bronchial veins to the azygos system.","Vagus nerve and its recurrent laryngeal branches (secretion, smooth muscle tone, cough reflex afferents) and sympathetic fibres from the T1-T4 region via the sympathetic trunk (airway relaxation).")
BR="Bronchial arteries, from the thoracic aorta (usually two on the left, one or two on the right, often with an intercostal origin), supply the airway walls and lung connective tissue. The pulmonary arteries carry deoxygenated blood to the alveoli for gas exchange."
BV="Bronchial veins drain to the azygos vein (right) and the accessory hemiazygos or left superior intercostal vein (left); much of the bronchial blood also reaches the pulmonary veins. Oxygenated blood from the alveoli returns through the pulmonary veins to the left atrium."
PP="Pulmonary plexus (anterior and posterior) at the lung root: vagus nerve parasympathetic fibres (bronchoconstriction, mucus secretion) and sympathetic fibres from T2-T5 (bronchodilation). Visceral afferents travel with the vagus; cough and stretch receptors are in the airway walls."
E('right-main-bronchus',BR,BV,PP)
E('left-main-bronchus',BR,BV,PP)
E('right-lung',BR,BV,PP+" The visceral pleura has no pain fibres.")
E('left-lung',BR,BV,PP+" The visceral pleura has no pain fibres.")
for L in ('right-upper-lobe','right-middle-lobe','right-lower-lobe','left-upper-lobe','left-lower-lobe'):
    E(L,BR+" Each bronchopulmonary segment has its own segmental bronchus and pulmonary artery branch.",BV+" Intersegmental veins run in the septa between segments.",PP)
E('alveoli',"Pulmonary capillaries fed by pulmonary arteries and arterioles form the dense gas-exchange network; bronchial arteries supply the airways only as far as the respiratory bronchioles.","Pulmonary venules and veins in the intersegmental septa, then the four pulmonary veins to the left atrium.","Alveolar walls have very little direct innervation. The airways and pulmonary vessels around them are controlled by the pulmonary plexus (vagal and sympathetic); stretch and irritant (C-fibre) receptors send afferents in the vagus nerve.")
E('alveolar-duct',"Pulmonary capillary network (alveolar walls); bronchial arteries end at about the respiratory bronchioles.","Pulmonary venules in the septa, then the pulmonary veins.","Pulmonary plexus (vagal and sympathetic) controls the smooth muscle at the duct openings.")
E('respiratory-bronchiole',"Terminal branches of bronchial arteries and the start of the pulmonary capillary network of the first alveoli.","Bronchial venules and pulmonary venules in the septa, then the pulmonary veins.","Pulmonary plexus: vagal (bronchoconstriction) and sympathetic (bronchodilation) fibres to the smooth muscle.")
E('bronchioles',"Bronchial arteries supply the walls down to the terminal bronchioles; pulmonary capillaries take over beyond.","Bronchial venules and pulmonary venules, then bronchial veins to the azygos system and pulmonary veins to the left atrium.","Pulmonary plexus: vagal parasympathetic fibres cause bronchoconstriction and sympathetic fibres from T2-T5 cause bronchodilation (beta-2 receptors).")
E('bronchial-tree',BR,BV,PP)
E('pulmonary-capillaries',"Fed by pulmonary arterioles carrying deoxygenated blood from the right ventricle through the pulmonary trunk and pulmonary arteries.","Pulmonary venules, then pulmonary veins to the left atrium.","Weak vasomotor fibres from the pulmonary plexus (vagal and sympathetic); pulmonary vessel tone is controlled mainly locally, by alveolar oxygen level (hypoxic vasoconstriction).")
E('pulmonary-arteriole',"The vessel carries deoxygenated blood from the right ventricle; its own wall is nourished by luminal blood and the vasa vasorum of the bronchial arteries (larger vessels).","Drains into the pulmonary capillaries it feeds.","Sparse sympathetic fibres from the pulmonary plexus; tone is mainly controlled locally (hypoxic vasoconstriction).")
E('pulmonary-venule',"The vessel carries oxygenated blood to the pulmonary veins; its wall is nourished by luminal blood and small bronchial vessels.","Drains into the pulmonary veins and then the left atrium.","Sparse sympathetic fibres from the pulmonary plexus.")
E('type-ii-pneumocyte',"A cell in the alveolar wall; it is supplied by the surrounding alveolar capillary network (pulmonary circulation).","Alveolar capillaries drain to pulmonary venules and veins.","A single cell has no nerve supply of its own; its surfactant secretion is regulated by stretch, hormones and local signals, with the airway nerves (vagal and sympathetic) acting on the surrounding tissue.")
E('alveolar-macrophage',"A free cell on the alveolar surface; it is nourished by the alveolar fluid and the neighbouring alveolar capillaries.","Not applicable; the neighbouring capillaries drain to pulmonary veins.","A free cell with no nerve supply; the surrounding airway tissue has vagal and sympathetic innervation.")
E('pleura',"Parietal pleura: intercostal arteries, internal thoracic and musculophrenic arteries, superior phrenic arteries. Visceral pleura: bronchial arteries (and pulmonary circulation).","Parietal pleura drains to the intercostal veins (azygos system) and internal thoracic veins; visceral pleura to the pulmonary veins.","Parietal pleura is pain-sensitive: costal and peripheral diaphragmatic parts by the intercostal nerves, central diaphragmatic and mediastinal parts by the phrenic nerve (C3-C5, pain referred to the shoulder). Visceral pleura is supplied by the pulmonary plexus and is insensitive to pain.")
LC="Hyaline cartilage is avascular: it is nourished through its perichondrium, which receives the laryngeal arteries (superior laryngeal from the superior thyroid, inferior laryngeal from the inferior thyroid, cricothyroid branch)."
LV="Superior and inferior laryngeal veins to the thyroid veins (internal jugular and brachiocephalic)."
LN="The cartilage itself has no named nerve; the overlying mucosa is supplied by the internal laryngeal nerve (above the vocal folds) and the recurrent laryngeal nerve (below), and the attached muscles by the recurrent laryngeal nerve (and the external laryngeal nerve for cricothyroid)."
E('thyroid-cartilage',LC+" The thyroid gland and the cricothyroid artery lie nearby.",LV,LN)
E('cricoid-cartilage',LC+" The cricothyroid branch of the superior thyroid artery crosses its front.",LV,LN)
E('arytenoid-cartilage',LC,LV,LN+" All intrinsic muscles that move it (except cricothyroid) are supplied by the recurrent laryngeal nerve.")
E('corniculate-cartilage',LC,LV,LN)
E('epiglottis',"The elastic cartilage is nourished through its perichondrium; the mucosa receives the superior laryngeal artery (internal branch) and lingual artery (epiglottic branches).","Superior laryngeal vein and lingual vein, draining to the internal jugular vein.","Internal laryngeal nerve (vagus X) for the laryngeal surface and glossopharyngeal nerve (IX) for the vallecular (lingual) surface; also carries taste buds on its mucosa (vagus). Sympathetic fibres from the superior cervical ganglion.")
E('uvula',"Ascending palatine artery (facial), lesser palatine arteries (from the descending palatine) and ascending pharyngeal artery.","Pharyngeal and pterygoid venous plexuses.","Musculus uvulae: pharyngeal plexus (vagus, cranial accessory fibres); sensory: lesser palatine nerves (V2) and glossopharyngeal nerve (IX).")
E('soft-palate',"Ascending palatine artery (facial), lesser palatine arteries (descending palatine of the maxillary), and ascending pharyngeal artery.","Pharyngeal and pterygoid venous plexuses.","Motor: pharyngeal plexus (vagus, cranial accessory fibres) to all muscles except tensor veli palatini (nerve to medial pterygoid, V3). Sensory: lesser palatine nerves (V2); the oropharyngeal surface by the glossopharyngeal nerve (IX). Taste: lesser palatine, greater petrosal.")
E('hard-palate',"Greater palatine artery (descending palatine of the maxillary artery) through the greater palatine foramen; the nasopalatine (sphenopalatine) branch through the incisive canal supplies the front.","Palatine veins to the pterygoid venous plexus.","Greater palatine nerve (maxillary V2) for most of the palate; nasopalatine nerve (V2) for the area behind the incisors.")
E('gingiva',"Upper: posterior, middle and anterior superior alveolar arteries and greater palatine artery. Lower: inferior alveolar artery (mental and incisive branches), buccal artery and lingual artery.","Corresponding veins to the pterygoid venous plexus and the facial vein.","Upper buccal gingiva: superior alveolar nerves (V2) and infraorbital nerve; palatal: greater palatine and nasopalatine nerves. Lower buccal gingiva: buccal nerve and mental nerve (V3); lingual gingiva: lingual nerve (V3).")
E('parotid-gland',"External carotid artery and its branches within the gland (superficial temporal and transverse facial arteries).","Retromandibular vein, which joins the posterior auricular vein to form the external jugular vein.","Parasympathetic secretomotor: glossopharyngeal nerve (IX) via the lesser petrosal nerve and otic ganglion to the auriculotemporal nerve. Sympathetic: plexus on the external carotid artery from the superior cervical ganglion. Sensory: great auricular nerve (capsule) and auriculotemporal nerve.")
E('submandibular-gland',"Facial artery (glandular branches), submental artery and lingual artery.","Facial vein and lingual veins to the internal jugular vein.","Parasympathetic secretomotor: facial nerve (VII) via the chorda tympani and lingual nerve to the submandibular ganglion. Sympathetic: plexus on the facial artery from the superior cervical ganglion.")
E('sublingual-gland',"Sublingual artery (from the lingual) and submental artery (from the facial).","Sublingual and submental veins.","Parasympathetic secretomotor: facial nerve (VII) via the chorda tympani and lingual nerve to the submandibular ganglion. Sympathetic: plexus on the facial artery.")
E('oral-cavity',"Branches of the external carotid artery: facial, lingual and maxillary (superior and inferior alveolar, buccal, palatine) arteries.","Facial, lingual and pterygoid venous plexus, draining to the internal jugular vein.","Sensory: maxillary (V2) and mandibular (V3) divisions of the trigeminal nerve; taste by the facial (chorda tympani) and glossopharyngeal nerves. Motor: V3 to the muscles of mastication, hypoglossal nerve to the tongue. Secretomotor to the salivary glands by VII and IX.")
E('tongue',"Lingual artery (dorsal lingual, deep lingual and sublingual branches) from the external carotid artery; minor supply from the tonsillar branch of the facial and the ascending pharyngeal artery.","Dorsal lingual and deep lingual veins drain to the internal jugular vein, directly or through the facial vein.","Motor: hypoglossal nerve (XII) to all intrinsic and extrinsic muscles except palatoglossus (pharyngeal plexus, vagus). General sensation: lingual nerve (V3) anterior two-thirds, glossopharyngeal (IX) posterior third. Taste: chorda tympani (VII) anterior two-thirds, glossopharyngeal (IX) posterior third, vagus at the epiglottis.")
E('taste-buds',"Lingual artery (and facial and ascending pharyngeal branches for the palate and pharynx).","Lingual veins to the internal jugular vein.","Chorda tympani (facial nerve VII) from fungiform papillae, glossopharyngeal nerve (IX) from circumvallate and foliate papillae, vagus nerve (X, internal laryngeal) from the epiglottis and palate.")
E('olfactory-epithelium',v="Sphenopalatine vein to the pterygoid plexus and anterior ethmoidal veins to the superior ophthalmic vein; the olfactory nerve fibres pass to the olfactory bulb with their own meningeal sheaths.")
E('ear',v="Veins accompany the arteries: posterior auricular and retromandibular veins (outer ear), tympanic and pterygoid veins (middle ear), labyrinthine vein to the inferior petrosal and sigmoid sinuses (inner ear).")
E('external-ear',v="Superficial temporal and posterior auricular veins to the retromandibular and external jugular veins.")
E('auricle',"Posterior auricular artery (cranial surface and most of the lateral surface) and anterior auricular branches of the superficial temporal artery (anterior part).","Superficial temporal and posterior auricular veins, draining to the retromandibular and external jugular veins.","Great auricular nerve (C2-C3) to most of the cranial surface and the lower lateral surface; auriculotemporal nerve (V3) to the anterior part; lesser occipital nerve (C2) to the upper cranial surface; auricular branch of the vagus (Arnold nerve) to the concha; facial nerve (small area at the concha). The extrinsic muscles are supplied by the temporal and posterior auricular branches of the facial nerve.")
E('tympanic-membrane',"Outer surface: deep auricular artery (maxillary). Inner (mucosal) surface: anterior tympanic artery (maxillary) and stylomastoid artery (posterior auricular).","Veins drain to the external jugular vein and the pterygoid venous plexus.","Outer surface: auriculotemporal nerve (V3) and the auricular branch of the vagus nerve (X). Inner mucosal surface: tympanic plexus from the tympanic branch of the glossopharyngeal nerve (IX, Jacobson nerve).")
E('cochlea',"Labyrinthine (internal auditory) artery, usually from the anterior inferior cerebellar artery, giving the common cochlear artery (a functional end artery).","Labyrinthine veins to the inferior petrosal sinus (through the cochlear aqueduct) or the sigmoid sinus.","Cochlear part of the vestibulocochlear nerve (VIII): afferent fibres from the spiral ganglion. Efferent olivocochlear fibres modulate the hair cells. Sympathetic fibres run with the vessels.")
E('vestibule',"Labyrinthine artery: anterior vestibular and vestibulocochlear branches; the stylomastoid branch of the posterior auricular artery supplies the posterior semicircular canal.","Labyrinthine veins to the inferior petrosal and sigmoid sinuses.","Vestibular part of the vestibulocochlear nerve (VIII) from the vestibular (Scarpa) ganglion; efferent fibres modulate hair cells.")
E('sclera',"Anterior and posterior ciliary arteries (branches of the ophthalmic artery); the sclera is poorly vascularised and is supplied mainly through the episcleral vessels.","Episcleral veins and vorticose veins to the ophthalmic veins.","Long and short posterior ciliary nerves (branches of the nasociliary nerve, V1).")
E('cornea',"Avascular: nourished by aqueous humour, the tear film and the limbal capillary arcades (anterior ciliary arteries).","Limbal veins to the episcleral veins; the cornea itself has no vessels.","Long ciliary nerves (nasociliary branch of the ophthalmic nerve, V1): very dense sensory fibres, giving the blink reflex and pain.")
E('iris',"Major arterial circle of the iris, formed by the long posterior and anterior ciliary arteries (ophthalmic artery).","Iris and ciliary veins to the vorticose veins and then the superior and inferior ophthalmic veins.","Parasympathetic: oculomotor nerve (III) via the ciliary ganglion and short ciliary nerves to sphincter pupillae. Sympathetic: superior cervical ganglion via long ciliary nerves to dilator pupillae. Sensory: long ciliary nerves (V1).")
E('pupil',"An aperture, not a tissue, so it has no supply of its own; the iris around it is supplied by the major arterial circle (long posterior and anterior ciliary arteries).","Not applicable; the iris veins drain to the vorticose veins.","Its size is set by the iris muscles: parasympathetic oculomotor nerve (III, via the ciliary ganglion) constricts it and sympathetic fibres from the superior cervical ganglion dilate it.")
E('lens',"Avascular. Nourished by the aqueous and vitreous humours; the fetal hyaloid artery regresses before birth.","Not applicable (no vessels).","The lens has no nerve supply. Its focus (accommodation) is changed by the ciliary muscle, supplied by parasympathetic fibres of the oculomotor nerve (III).")
E('zonular-fibres',"Acellular fibres with no blood vessels; bathed by aqueous humour from the ciliary body.","Not applicable.","No nerve supply of their own. Their tension is changed by the ciliary muscle, supplied by parasympathetic fibres of the oculomotor nerve (III).")
E('anterior-chamber',"Contains aqueous humour, no vessels; the humour is secreted by the ciliary body, which is supplied by the long posterior and anterior ciliary arteries.","Aqueous drains through the trabecular meshwork and the canal of Schlemm into the episcleral veins.","Contains no nerves; the surrounding iris and ciliary body are supplied by short and long ciliary nerves (sympathetic and parasympathetic).")
E('vitreous-body',"Avascular; nourished by the surrounding retina and ciliary body (the fetal hyaloid artery regresses).","Not applicable.","None; a gel with no cells that need a nerve supply.")
E('retina',"Central retinal artery (an end artery) supplies the inner layers; the choriocapillaris (short posterior ciliary arteries) supplies the outer layers including the photoreceptors. The fovea is supplied by the choroid alone.","Central retinal vein to the superior ophthalmic vein or directly to the cavernous sinus.","Optic nerve (II): afferent fibres from the retinal ganglion cells. The retinal vessels have no autonomic supply and are controlled by local autoregulation.")
E('choroid',v="Vorticose veins to the superior and inferior ophthalmic veins.",n="Sympathetic fibres from the superior cervical ganglion (vasomotor) and parasympathetic fibres via the short ciliary nerves; sensory fibres in the ciliary nerves (V1).")
E('macula-fovea',"Choriocapillaris (short posterior ciliary arteries); a cilioretinal artery in some people; the fovea has no retinal vessels (foveal avascular zone).","Vorticose veins; central retinal vein for the perifoveal retina.","Afferent fibres through the optic nerve (II); no autonomic nerves to the vessels (local autoregulation).")
E('optic-disc',"Central retinal artery and the short posterior ciliary arteries (circle of Zinn-Haller).","Central retinal vein.","The exit of the optic nerve (II); has no photoreceptors (the blind spot). Its vessels are autoregulated.")
E('lacrimal-gland',"Lacrimal artery (branch of the ophthalmic artery).","Lacrimal vein to the superior ophthalmic vein.","Parasympathetic secretomotor: facial nerve (VII) via the greater petrosal nerve, pterygopalatine ganglion and zygomatic nerve to the lacrimal nerve (V1). Sympathetic: deep petrosal nerve and carotid plexus. Sensory: lacrimal nerve (V1).")
E('lacrimal-apparatus',"Medial palpebral arteries, angular artery (facial) and dorsal nasal artery (ophthalmic).","Angular vein (to the facial vein) and the ophthalmic veins.","Infratrochlear nerve (V1) to the sac and canaliculi; anterior superior alveolar nerve (V2) to the nasolacrimal duct; orbicularis oculi (lacrimal part) by the facial nerve (VII) pumps tears.")
E('tarsal-plates',"Marginal and peripheral arterial arcades of the eyelid, from the medial palpebral (ophthalmic) and lateral palpebral (lacrimal) arteries.","Palpebral veins to the superficial temporal, facial and ophthalmic veins.","Sensory: upper lid by the supraorbital, supratrochlear, lacrimal and infratrochlear nerves (V1); lower lid by the infraorbital nerve (V2). Sympathetic fibres run to the superior tarsal muscle (Muller).")
E('eye',"Ophthalmic artery (from the internal carotid artery): central retinal artery to the inner retina, and long, short and anterior ciliary arteries to the choroid, ciliary body, iris and sclera.","Superior and inferior ophthalmic veins to the cavernous sinus; central retinal vein and vorticose veins.","Optic nerve (II) for vision. Oculomotor (III), trochlear (IV) and abducens (VI) nerves to the extraocular muscles. Parasympathetic fibres of III (via the ciliary ganglion) to sphincter pupillae and ciliary muscle; sympathetic fibres (superior cervical ganglion) to dilator pupillae and the tarsal muscle. Sensory: nasociliary nerve (V1).")
E('vulva',"External pudendal arteries (from the femoral) anteriorly; internal pudendal branches (posterior labial, perineal, artery of the bulb, clitoral arteries) posteriorly.","External pudendal veins to the great saphenous vein; internal pudendal veins to the internal iliac vein.","Anterior: ilioinguinal nerve and the genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve (L1-L2). Posterior: perineal and posterior labial branches of the pudendal nerve (S2-S4); dorsal nerve of the clitoris; perineal branch of the posterior femoral cutaneous nerve.")
E('mons-pubis',"External pudendal arteries (femoral).","External pudendal veins to the great saphenous vein.","Ilioinguinal nerve and the genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve (L1-L2); iliohypogastric nerve from above.")
E('labia-majora',"Anteriorly the external pudendal arteries; posteriorly the posterior labial branches of the internal pudendal artery.","External pudendal veins (to the great saphenous vein) and internal pudendal veins.","Anterior: ilioinguinal nerve and genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve (L1-L2). Posterior: labial branches of the perineal nerve (pudendal, S2-S4) and the perineal branch of the posterior femoral cutaneous nerve.")
E('labia-minora',"Posterior labial branches of the internal pudendal artery, with branches of the external pudendal arteries.","Labial veins to the internal pudendal and external pudendal veins.","Pudendal nerve (S2-S4) through the perineal and posterior labial branches; ilioinguinal nerve anteriorly. Rich sensory endings.")
E('clitoris',"Deep and dorsal arteries of the clitoris and the artery of the bulb of the vestibule (branches of the internal pudendal artery).","Deep dorsal vein to the vesical venous plexus; superficial dorsal vein to the external pudendal vein.","Dorsal nerve of the clitoris (pudendal nerve, S2-S4) for sensation; cavernous nerves from the uterovaginal plexus (parasympathetic S2-S4, sympathetic from T10-L2) for erection.")
E('vaginal-opening',"Vaginal artery, branches of the internal pudendal artery (posterior labial, artery of the vestibular bulb).","Vaginal venous plexus and internal pudendal veins to the internal iliac vein.","Pudendal nerve (S2-S4) through the perineal and posterior labial branches; the introitus is pain-sensitive.")
E('vagina',"Vaginal artery (internal iliac), cervicovaginal branch of the uterine artery, and branches of the internal pudendal and middle rectal arteries to the lower part.","Vaginal venous plexus to the internal iliac vein.","Upper two-thirds: uterovaginal plexus (inferior hypogastric plexus; sympathetic L1-L2, parasympathetic S2-S4), insensitive to touch. Lower third: pudendal nerve (S2-S4), sensitive to touch and pain.")
E('cervix',"Cervical and vaginal branches of the uterine artery, with the vaginal artery.","Uterine and vaginal venous plexus to the internal iliac vein.","Uterovaginal plexus (inferior hypogastric plexus). Visceral afferents with the pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2-S4); the cervix is relatively insensitive to cutting but responds to stretch.")
E('uterus',"Uterine artery (internal iliac), reaching the uterus in the broad ligament and anastomosing with the ovarian artery; arcuate, radial and spiral arteries within the wall.","Uterine venous plexus to the uterine veins and then the internal iliac veins; a part drains with the ovarian veins.","Uterovaginal plexus (inferior hypogastric plexus): sympathetic (T10-L2) and parasympathetic (pelvic splanchnic nerves, S2-S4). Fundus and body pain with sympathetic afferents (T10-L1); cervix and lower uterus with S2-S4.")
E('uterine-tube',"Tubal branches of the uterine and ovarian arteries, which anastomose in the mesosalpinx.","Uterine and ovarian (pampiniform) veins.","Ovarian and uterovaginal plexuses; visceral afferents with sympathetic fibres (T11-L1) and pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2-S4).")
E('fimbriae-of-uterine-tube',"Tubal branches of the ovarian and uterine arteries.","Ovarian (pampiniform) and uterine veins.","Ovarian plexus (sympathetic fibres with the ovarian vessels) and uterovaginal plexus; visceral afferents with sympathetic fibres.")
E('ovary',"Ovarian artery (from the abdominal aorta at L2) through the suspensory ligament, anastomosing with the ovarian branch of the uterine artery.","Pampiniform plexus to the ovarian vein: right to the inferior vena cava, left to the left renal vein.","Ovarian plexus (sympathetic fibres with the ovarian vessels, from T10-T11 through the aortic and renal plexuses). Visceral afferents with sympathetic fibres; pain is felt at T10.")
E('mammary-gland',"Lateral thoracic and thoracoacromial branches of the axillary artery; perforating branches of the internal thoracic artery (2nd-4th spaces) medially; posterior intercostal branches.","Mainly the axillary vein; also internal thoracic and posterior intercostal veins to the azygos system.","Anterior and lateral cutaneous branches of the 4th-6th intercostal nerves (T3-T6; the nipple at T4); sympathetic fibres for vasomotor control. Milk production is controlled by hormones, not nerves.")
E('testis',"Testicular artery (from the abdominal aorta below the renal arteries), anastomosing with the cremasteric artery and the artery of the ductus deferens.","Pampiniform plexus to the testicular vein: right to the inferior vena cava, left to the left renal vein.","Testicular plexus (sympathetic T10-T11 with the testicular vessels, through the renal and aortic plexuses). Visceral afferents at T10; the coverings are supplied by the genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve (L1-L2) and ilioinguinal nerve.")
E('epididymis',"Testicular artery and the artery of the ductus deferens (from the inferior vesical artery).","Pampiniform plexus to the testicular vein.","Testicular plexus and pelvic plexus (sympathetic fibres from T10-L2).")
E('spermatic-cord',"Testicular artery, cremasteric artery (inferior epigastric) and artery of the ductus deferens (inferior vesical).","Pampiniform plexus to the testicular vein (right to IVC, left to left renal vein); cremasteric veins to the inferior epigastric vein.","Genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve (L1-L2) to cremaster; ilioinguinal nerve (sensory, along the cord); sympathetic fibres with the vessels from the testicular and pelvic plexuses.")
E('scrotum',"Anterior scrotal branches of the external pudendal arteries; posterior scrotal branches of the internal pudendal artery; cremasteric artery to the coverings.","External pudendal and internal pudendal veins.","Anterior: ilioinguinal nerve and genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve (L1-L2). Posterior: scrotal branches of the perineal nerve (pudendal, S2-S4) and the perineal branch of the posterior femoral cutaneous nerve (S3). Sympathetic fibres to the dartos muscle.")
E('prostate',"Inferior vesical artery (urethral and capsular branches), with middle rectal and internal pudendal branches.","Prostatic venous plexus to the internal iliac veins; it communicates with the vertebral venous plexus.","Prostatic plexus (inferior hypogastric plexus): sympathetic fibres (from T10-L2) cause contraction at emission, parasympathetic fibres (S2-S4) control secretion. The cavernous nerves run along the capsule.")
E('seminal-vesicle',"Inferior vesical and middle rectal arteries, with the artery of the ductus deferens.","Prostatic and vesical venous plexus to the internal iliac veins.","Inferior hypogastric plexus: sympathetic fibres (T10-L2) cause emission; parasympathetic fibres (S2-S4).")
E('penis',"Internal pudendal artery: dorsal artery, deep artery (to the corpora cavernosa) and artery of the bulb; superficial coverings by the external pudendal arteries.","Superficial dorsal vein to the great saphenous vein; deep dorsal vein to the prostatic plexus; cavernous veins.","Dorsal nerve of the penis (pudendal nerve, S2-S4) for sensation; cavernous nerves from the prostatic plexus: parasympathetic (S2-S4) for erection, sympathetic (T10-L2) for ejaculation and detumescence. Ilioinguinal nerve to the root.")
E('corpora-cavernosa',"Deep artery of the penis (internal pudendal) with helicine arteries into the erectile spaces.","Emissary veins to the deep dorsal vein and the circumflex veins.","Cavernous nerves from the prostatic plexus: parasympathetic fibres (pelvic splanchnic nerves, S2-S4) cause vasodilation and erection; sympathetic fibres cause detumescence.")
E('corpus-spongiosum',"Artery of the bulb of the penis and the urethral artery (internal pudendal), with branches of the dorsal artery.","Bulbar veins to the internal pudendal and prostatic plexus; veins of the glans to the deep dorsal vein.","Perineal nerve and dorsal nerve of the penis (pudendal nerve, S2-S4); cavernous nerves (autonomic).")
E('glans-penis',"Terminal branches of the dorsal arteries of the penis and the corpus spongiosum.","Venous plexus of the corona to the deep dorsal vein of the penis.","Dorsal nerve of the penis (pudendal nerve, S2-S4): very rich sensory endings.")
E('kidney',n="Renal plexus (sympathetic fibres from T10-L1 through the lesser and least splanchnic nerves and the aorticorenal ganglion): controls vessel tone and renin release; minor vagal fibres. Visceral afferents from T10-L1; pain is felt from the loin to the groin.")
E('renal-pelvis',"Branches of the renal artery (pelvic and ureteric branches).","Renal vein.","Renal plexus: sympathetic fibres (T10-L1) and visceral afferents; distension causes renal colic, felt from the loin to the groin.")
E('ureter',"Renal artery (upper part), gonadal artery and abdominal aortic branches (middle part), and common iliac, internal iliac, vesical and uterine or inferior vesical arteries (lower part); the arteries reach it from the nearest vessels and form a longitudinal network.","Veins accompany the arteries to the renal, gonadal, iliac and vesical veins.","Sympathetic fibres (T10-L2) through the renal, aortic and hypogastric plexuses; parasympathetic fibres from S2-S4 pelvic splanchnic nerves. Visceral afferents with the sympathetic nerves: ureteric colic is felt from the loin to the groin, testis or labia.")
E('urinary-bladder',"Superior vesical arteries (from the umbilical artery) and inferior vesical arteries (or vaginal arteries in women), with branches of the obturator and inferior gluteal arteries.","Vesical venous plexus to the internal iliac veins.","Parasympathetic: pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2-S4) contract the detrusor (micturition). Sympathetic: T11-L2 through the inferior hypogastric plexus (relaxes the detrusor, contracts the internal sphincter in men). Somatic: pudendal nerve (S2-S4) to the external urethral sphincter. Visceral afferents: stretch (S2-S4) and pain.")
E('urethra',"Inferior vesical (or vaginal) arteries and internal pudendal artery branches.","Vesical venous plexus and internal pudendal veins.","Autonomic fibres from the vesical plexus; sensation and the external urethral sphincter by the pudendal nerve (S2-S4).")
E('male-urethra',"Prostatic part: inferior vesical and middle rectal arteries. Membranous part: branches of the internal pudendal artery. Spongy part: artery of the bulb and urethral artery (internal pudendal) and dorsal artery of the penis.","Prostatic venous plexus and internal pudendal veins, to the internal iliac veins.","Prostatic plexus (inferior hypogastric plexus; sympathetic T10-L2, parasympathetic S2-S4); pudendal nerve (S2-S4) through the perineal and dorsal penile nerves to the spongy part and the external sphincter.")
E('external-urethral-orifice',"Branches of the internal pudendal artery (perineal and vestibular arteries).","Internal pudendal veins and the vesical venous plexus.","Pudendal nerve (S2-S4), perineal branches: sensory.")
E('adrenal-gland',"Superior suprarenal arteries (inferior phrenic), middle suprarenal arteries (aorta) and inferior suprarenal arteries (renal artery).","Right suprarenal vein drains directly to the inferior vena cava; the left drains to the left renal vein.","Coeliac plexus and splanchnic nerves (greater, lesser, least). Pre-ganglionic sympathetic fibres (T10-L1) go straight to the chromaffin cells of the medulla, with no ganglion relay. No parasympathetic supply. Visceral afferents with sympathetic fibres.")
E('pituitary-gland',"Superior hypophysial arteries (from the supraclinoid internal carotid artery) supply the hypophyseal portal system to the anterior lobe; inferior hypophysial arteries (from the meningohypophyseal trunk) supply the posterior lobe.","Hypophyseal portal veins carry hypothalamic hormones to the anterior lobe; hormones leave through the hypophyseal veins to the cavernous sinuses.","The anterior lobe has no secretory nerve supply and is controlled by hypothalamic releasing hormones in the portal blood. The posterior lobe is an extension of the hypothalamus: the hypothalamo-hypophyseal tract. Sympathetic fibres from the carotid plexus run to its vessels.")
E('thyroid-gland',"Superior thyroid artery (external carotid) and inferior thyroid artery (thyrocervical trunk); sometimes a thyroid ima artery from the brachiocephalic trunk.","Superior and middle thyroid veins to the internal jugular vein; inferior thyroid veins to the brachiocephalic veins.","Sympathetic fibres from the superior, middle and inferior cervical ganglia (vasomotor), through the periarterial plexuses; the gland itself is controlled by TSH. The recurrent laryngeal and external laryngeal nerves lie close and are at risk in surgery.")
E('parathyroid-glands',"Inferior thyroid arteries, with branches from the superior thyroid arteries.","Thyroid veins (superior, middle and inferior).","Sympathetic fibres from the cervical ganglia (vasomotor); secretion is controlled by blood calcium level, not by nerves.")
E('spleen',"Splenic artery (coeliac trunk), dividing into hilar branches; short gastric arteries run to the stomach.","Splenic vein, which joins the superior mesenteric vein behind the neck of the pancreas to form the portal vein.","Splenic (coeliac) plexus: sympathetic fibres (T6-T10) with the splenic artery, controlling vessel tone and capsule contraction; minor vagal fibres. Visceral afferents with sympathetic nerves; peritoneal irritation can refer pain to the left shoulder through the phrenic nerve.")
E('thymus',"Inferior thyroid artery and branches of the internal thoracic artery (thymic branches).","Left brachiocephalic vein, internal thoracic and inferior thyroid veins.","Vagus nerve branches and sympathetic fibres from the cervical and stellate ganglia; the phrenic nerve supplies the capsule.")
E('palatine-tonsils',"Tonsillar branch of the facial artery (main supply), with ascending palatine, ascending pharyngeal, dorsal lingual and lesser palatine arteries.","Paratonsillar (external palatine) vein to the pharyngeal venous plexus and facial vein; a source of bleeding after tonsillectomy.","Tonsillar branches of the glossopharyngeal nerve (IX) and the lesser palatine nerves (V2); IX can refer pain to the ear.")
E('skin',"Three arterial plexuses: subcutaneous, cutaneous (reticular dermal) and subpapillary, fed by fasciocutaneous and musculocutaneous perforating arteries.","Venous plexuses parallel to the arterial plexuses, draining to the superficial veins.","Dermatomal sensory supply by cutaneous branches of spinal nerves (the trigeminal nerve for the face). Sympathetic fibres (post-ganglionic) to sweat glands, arrector pili muscles and blood vessels. No parasympathetic supply.")
E('epidermis',"Avascular: nourished by diffusion from the capillary loops in the papillary dermis.","Not applicable; the dermal venules drain the area.","Free nerve endings from the dermal plexus extend into the epidermis; Merkel cell endings. No motor supply.")
E('dermis',"Capillary loops of the papillary dermis from the subpapillary plexus; deeper reticular dermis from the cutaneous arterial plexus.","Venous plexus of the dermis parallel to the arterial plexuses.","Sensory receptors (Meissner, Ruffini and Pacinian corpuscles, free endings) from the cutaneous nerves; sympathetic fibres to arteriovenous anastomoses, sweat glands and arrector pili.")
E('hypodermis',"Subcutaneous arterial plexus from fasciocutaneous and musculocutaneous perforating arteries.","Superficial veins of the region (such as the cephalic, basilic and great saphenous veins).","Cutaneous nerve branches run through it; Pacinian corpuscles; sympathetic fibres to blood vessels and fat tissue.")
E('skin-appendages',"Dermal capillary plexus around each hair follicle and gland; the nail bed is supplied by the digital arteries.","Venous plexus of the dermis; digital veins for the nail.","Eccrine sweat glands: sympathetic cholinergic fibres. Apocrine glands: sympathetic adrenergic fibres. Arrector pili: sympathetic fibres. Sebaceous glands: hormonal control. Hair follicles have sensory nerve endings; nails by digital nerves.")
E('stomach',"Left gastric artery (coeliac trunk), right gastric artery (hepatic), right and left gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) arteries, short gastric arteries and posterior gastric arteries (splenic).","Left and right gastric veins and the short gastric and gastro-omental veins drain to the portal vein through the splenic and superior mesenteric veins.","Parasympathetic: anterior and posterior vagal trunks (X) increase motility and acid secretion. Sympathetic: coeliac plexus (T6-T9 via the greater splanchnic nerve), vasomotor and pyloric sphincter control. Visceral pain afferents with the sympathetic nerves (epigastric pain).")
E('fundus-of-stomach',"Short gastric arteries and posterior gastric arteries (splenic artery), with the left gastro-omental artery.","Short gastric veins to the splenic vein and the portal vein.","Vagal trunks (X) and coeliac plexus (sympathetic, T6-T9).")
E('body-of-stomach',"Left and right gastric arteries along the lesser curvature; left and right gastro-omental arteries along the greater curvature.","Left and right gastric veins; left and right gastro-omental veins.","Anterior and posterior vagal trunks (X) with their gastric branches (parietal cell secretion) and coeliac plexus (sympathetic, T6-T9).")
E('pyloric-part-of-stomach',"Right gastric artery, right gastro-omental artery and supraduodenal branches of the gastroduodenal artery.","Right gastric vein to the portal vein; right gastro-omental vein to the superior mesenteric vein.","Vagal branches (hepatic and antral branches) and coeliac plexus; the pyloric sphincter has both sympathetic (closing) and vagal (relaxing) control.")
E('oesophagus',"Cervical part: inferior thyroid arteries. Thoracic part: oesophageal branches of the thoracic aorta and bronchial arteries. Abdominal part: left gastric and inferior phrenic arteries.","Cervical part to the inferior thyroid veins; thoracic part to the azygos and hemiazygos veins; abdominal part to the left gastric vein (portal system), a site of portal-systemic anastomosis.","Oesophageal plexus (vagus and sympathetic trunk, T4-T6). Upper striated third by the recurrent laryngeal nerve; lower smooth muscle part by vagal parasympathetic fibres. Visceral afferents carry oesophageal pain, felt behind the sternum.")
E('oesophagus-cervical-part',"Inferior thyroid arteries.","Inferior thyroid veins to the left brachiocephalic vein.","Recurrent laryngeal nerve (vagus) for striated muscle; sympathetic fibres from the cervical ganglia.")
E('oesophagus-thoracic-part',"Oesophageal branches of the thoracic aorta and bronchial arteries.","Azygos and hemiazygos veins.","Oesophageal plexus (vagus, parasympathetic, and thoracic sympathetic trunk T4-T6).")
E('oesophagus-abdominal-part',"Left gastric artery and left inferior phrenic artery.","Left gastric vein to the portal vein (portal-systemic anastomosis with the azygos system).","Oesophageal plexus and anterior and posterior vagal trunks (X); sympathetic fibres from the greater splanchnic nerve.")
E('liver',n="Hepatic plexus (coeliac plexus): sympathetic fibres (T7-T10 through the greater splanchnic nerve) with the hepatic artery, and parasympathetic fibres from the anterior vagal trunk. The capsule is pain-sensitive through the right phrenic nerve and lower intercostal nerves (pain felt in the right shoulder).")
E('gallbladder',"Cystic artery, usually from the right hepatic artery, found in the cystohepatic triangle (of Calot).","Cystic vein to the right branch of the portal vein, with small veins directly into the liver.","Coeliac plexus (sympathetic, T7-T9) and vagal fibres (parasympathetic, cholecystokinin response); visceral afferents with sympathetic nerves; the right phrenic nerve to the peritoneal surface refers pain to the right shoulder.")
E('pancreas',"Head: anterior and posterior superior and inferior pancreaticoduodenal arteries (gastroduodenal and superior mesenteric arteries). Body and tail: branches of the splenic artery (dorsal pancreatic, greater pancreatic and caudal pancreatic arteries).","Splenic vein and superior mesenteric vein, and pancreaticoduodenal veins, all to the portal vein.","Vagus nerve (parasympathetic, secretomotor); coeliac and superior mesenteric plexuses (sympathetic, T5-T9 through the splanchnic nerves) for vasoconstriction and pain afferents (felt in the epigastrium and back).")
E('small-intestine',"Duodenum: superior and inferior pancreaticoduodenal arteries. Jejunum and ileum: jejunal and ileal branches of the superior mesenteric artery (arcades and vasa recta); ileocolic artery for the terminal ileum.","Superior mesenteric vein to the portal vein.","Superior mesenteric plexus: parasympathetic from the vagus nerve; sympathetic from the lesser splanchnic nerve (T10-T11) via the superior mesenteric ganglion. The enteric nervous system lies in the wall. Pain from the midgut is felt in the periumbilical area.")
E('duodenum',"Above the major duodenal papilla: gastroduodenal and superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries (coeliac trunk); below it: inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery (superior mesenteric artery).","Pancreaticoduodenal veins to the superior mesenteric vein and the portal vein.","Vagus nerve (parasympathetic) and the coeliac and superior mesenteric plexuses (sympathetic, from the greater and lesser splanchnic nerves). Pain referred to the epigastrium.")
E('jejunum',"Jejunal branches of the superior mesenteric artery, forming arcades and long vasa recta.","Jejunal veins to the superior mesenteric vein.","Superior mesenteric plexus: vagal parasympathetic fibres and sympathetic fibres (T9-T10 through the lesser splanchnic nerve and superior mesenteric ganglion); myenteric and submucosal plexuses.")
E('ileum',"Ileal branches of the superior mesenteric artery (arcades and short vasa recta), and the ileal branch of the ileocolic artery for the terminal part.","Ileal veins to the superior mesenteric vein.","Superior mesenteric plexus: vagal parasympathetic fibres and sympathetic fibres (T9-T10 through the lesser splanchnic nerve and superior mesenteric ganglion); myenteric and submucosal plexuses.")
E('appendix',"Appendicular artery (branch of the ileocolic artery, from the superior mesenteric artery) in the mesoappendix; it is an end artery.","Appendicular vein to the ileocolic vein, superior mesenteric vein and portal vein.","Superior mesenteric plexus: sympathetic fibres (T10) and vagal parasympathetic fibres; visceral pain is felt around the umbilicus (T10), later localising to the right iliac fossa when the peritoneum is involved.")
E('large-intestine',"Caecum to the proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon: ileocolic, right colic and middle colic branches of the superior mesenteric artery. Distal one-third of the transverse colon to the sigmoid colon: left colic and sigmoid branches of the inferior mesenteric artery; the marginal artery links the two territories.","Superior mesenteric vein (right colon) and inferior mesenteric vein (left colon, to the splenic or superior mesenteric vein), draining to the portal vein.","Proximal part: superior mesenteric plexus (sympathetic from the least and lesser splanchnic nerves, vagal parasympathetic). Distal part: lumbar splanchnic nerves (L1-L2, sympathetic) through the inferior mesenteric plexus; pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2-S4) supply the parasympathetic fibres for the left colon and the rectum. The enteric nervous system lies in the wall.")
E('rectum',"Superior rectal artery (inferior mesenteric artery), middle rectal arteries (internal iliac) and inferior rectal arteries (internal pudendal).","Superior rectal vein to the inferior mesenteric vein and the portal vein; middle and inferior rectal veins to the internal iliac veins (portal-systemic anastomosis; haemorrhoids).","Sympathetic: lumbar splanchnic nerves (L1-L2) via the inferior mesenteric and hypogastric plexuses. Parasympathetic: pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2-S4) for motility and defecation. Sensation of stretch with the pelvic splanchnic nerves; below the pectinate line the inferior rectal nerve (pudendal) is sensitive to pain.")
